WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.382
From ripples to bubbles—and back.

2
00:00:02.382 --> 00:00:04.380
Start with the rippling surface.

3
00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:09.792
In this BHSM illustration, overlapping ripples reinforce into a rogue wave.

4
00:00:09.792 --> 00:00:14.831
A crest pinches off into a bubble, which travels with a surrounding pilot-wave pattern.

5
00:00:14.831 --> 00:00:17.653
A second bubble arrives and the two collide.

6
00:00:17.653 --> 00:00:23.890
The resulting daughter bubbles are unstable: they break apart, pop and settle back into surface ripples.

7
00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:29.367
The final spreading pattern represents the proposed return of residual inertia to the surface.

8
00:00:29.367 --> 00:00:36.919
This is a conceptual cycle, not a calculated energy transfer, measured decay channel or prediction of physical rates.

9
00:00:36.919 --> 00:00:39.509
Pause at any stage to follow the changes.

10
00:00:39.509 --> 00:00:43.452
Follow what changes at pinch-off, collision and the final pop.
